</ds-metadata-field-wrapper><!----><hr _ngcontent-sc865=""></ds-language-availability>
    </span>
    <div _ngcontent-sc866="" class="item-list-abstract mt-4 ng-star-inserted">
      <ds-truncatable-part _ngcontent-sc866="" background="gray-100" _nghost-sc202=""><div _ngcontent-sc202="" class="clamp-gray-100-3 min-3">
    <div _ngcontent-sc202="" class="content dont-break-out">
        <span _ngcontent-sc866="">Vanuatu's poultry sector combines widespread traditional backyard chicken-keeping predominantly led by women and youth. There is emerging semi-commercial layer and broiler industry near Port Vila and Luganville. While domestically produced frozen chicken cannot compete with cheaper imports, higher prices for fresh birds offer a viable, if limited, niche market (estimated 10,000–24,000 birds/month).Key constraints include heavy dependence on costly imported feed and day-old chicks, disrupted air freight since 2024, underutilized local feed ingredients, limited processing and cold-chain infrastructure, and a shortage of specialized veterinary and extension services.The analysis recommends a phased roadmap: piloting decentralized feed-processing units (Port Vila and Santo) under the current FAO Technical Cooperation Programme; community-based breeding to strengthen indigenous stock; extension and training system upgrades; market linkages with hotels and retailers; and establishing a National Poultry Task Force to coordinate policy and investment. With sustained support, Vanuatu could shift from import dependence toward a resilient, locally driven poultry sector by 2035.</span>
      
    </div>
</div>
</ds-truncatable-part>
    </div><!---->
